---
import ArticleLayout from "@layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import BlockEditorShowcase from "@components/BlockEditorShowcase.astro";
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Block Editor"
  description="An experiment taking Gutenberg out of WordPress and into a standalone desktop app, with pages saved as local JSON files."
  subtitle="What if the block editor had a place of its own?"
  eyebrow="2026 · Experiment / Desktop tools"
  tags="Gutenberg · Electron · React · TypeScript"
>
  <BlockEditorShowcase slot="art" loading="eager" />

  <p class="lead">
    The familiar WordPress editor, without the rest of WordPress. A small
    desktop experiment in composing with blocks and keeping the work on disk.
  </p>
  <a
    class="text-link"
    href="https://github.com/goodjobdev/block-editor"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
  >
    View the repository on GitHub ↗
  </a>

  <section>
    <h2>Just the editor</h2>
    <p>
      Gutenberg is a useful way to put a page together. This experiment takes
      its editor packages out of the WordPress admin and gives them a simpler
      home: a page list, an editing canvas and a block inspector inside an
      Electron app. There is no WordPress installation, PHP server or database
      to set up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>A small loop: create, write, save</h2>
    <p>
      Create a page, give it a title and start adding blocks. The prototype
      registers Gutenberg’s core blocks and includes a small collection of
      patterns, with List View for navigating the document and an inspector for
      block settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      Changes autosave after a short pause, with a Save button and Cmd/Ctrl+S
      for an explicit save. Returning to the page list saves the current page
      before leaving the editor.
    </p>
    <figure class="page-list-screenshot">
      <a href="/assets/block-editor/pages.jpg">
        <img
          src="/assets/block-editor/pages.jpg"
          alt="The prototype’s Pages screen showing a saved test page and the New Page button"
          width="1600"
          height="1000"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        The page list after saving a test document. Open the full screenshot.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Local files, not a publishing platform</h2>
    <p>
      Each page is a JSON file in the app’s local data directory. It holds the
      title, slug, timestamps and Gutenberg’s serialized block HTML, with a
      separate index for the page list. File reads and writes happen through
      Electron’s main process rather than an HTTP API.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is an editor experiment, not a replacement for a complete CMS.
      Publishing, sync and a managed media library are outside the current
      prototype.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Try it locally</h2>
    <p>
      The repository includes setup instructions and notes on the architecture.
      Clone it, install the npm dependencies and run the desktop app from
      source. React 18 and several WordPress package versions are deliberately
      pinned to keep the editor dependencies compatible.
    </p>
    <a
      class="text-link"
      href="https://github.com/goodjobdev/block-editor#quick-start"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Setup instructions on GitHub ↗
    </a>
  </section>

  <aside class="project-status">
    Working desktop experiment · These are screenshots of the prototype, not an
    embedded editor. No browser demo or packaged download is provided.
  </aside>
  <a class="text-link" href="/r%26d/">
    More experiments ↗
  </a>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .page-list-screenshot {
    margin: 28px 0 0;
  }
  .page-list-screenshot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
